                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9ecff90c-2a2d-3305-891b-60e0492816f6",
                        "entry_id": "518cbe90-c669-3485-a04c-57cf54dbab04",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A female given name from Ancient Greek, taken to regular use at the end of the 19th century."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cd187ce9-657c-4454-a90c-5bc01d6bb484",
                                "sense_id": "9ecff90c-2a2d-3305-891b-60e0492816f6",
                                "text": "\"My Doris—may I call you that, dearest?\"\n\"Call me Sappho, call me Chloris, call me Lalage, or Doris—only call me thine,\" I should have answered, if it had not been a little too sentimental.… I am afraid I omitted to state, in the proper place, that Doris is a name which has descended through a dozen generations of our family, that it belongs to myself as well as to my niece […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f27373c9-feeb-4f1c-a337-180bdaf954d6",
                                "sense_id": "9ecff90c-2a2d-3305-891b-60e0492816f6",
                                "text": "I've never met an old person named Judy. Now that's true. Maybe something happens to girls with young names like Debby, Judy, and Susie. At a certain age they make you change it to Doris, Edna, or Myrtle."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
